\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Translation blocks for a symmetric connection}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{On the complex field space with ordinary translations #{T_x},
let #{D_U} be the [centered operator](fdip-009O) for a unitary
connection #{U}. It preserves every
\vocabk{translation block}{fdip-016C} precisely when it commutes with
all #{T_x}. Suppose a group of coordinate changes acts on fields by invertible
complex-linear maps #{R_b}, including its supplied lifts on spin and
fiber. Assume
#{R_bR_c=R_{bc}}, #{R_b T_x R_b^{-1}=T_{bx}}, and
#{R_b D_U R_b^{-1}=D_{bU}} for the transported connections.
An element with #{bU=U} commutes with #{D_U} through #{R_b}.}

\p{More generally, suppose vertex gauge maps #{\mathcal G_b} identify #{bU}
with #{U}, so that #{\mathcal G_b D_{bU} \mathcal G_b^{-1}=D_U}. The corrected maps
#{\mathcal G_bR_b} commute with #{D_U}. They define a representation of
the coordinate group precisely when
##{\mathcal G_{bc}=\mathcal G_b R_b \mathcal G_c R_b^{-1}\quad\text{for all }b,c.}
For this representation to extend the given translations to the
semidirect product it must also satisfy
#{(\mathcal G_bR_b)T_x(\mathcal G_bR_b)^{-1}=T_{bx}} for all #{b,x}.}

\proof{
\p{Commuting with every translation gives commutation with each
finite projection in \ref{fdip-016E}, hence preservation of its
range. Conversely, the translations act by scalars on each block.
Preservation of the blocks gives commutation there, hence on their
direct sum. At trivial links the ordinary translations commute with
every centered difference, since the coordinate shifts commute.}

\p{The field covariance at a [vertex gauge](fdip-009O) is
\ref{fdip-009P}. Composing the two displayed covariance identities
gives the commutation of #{\mathcal G_bR_b} with #{D_U}. Multiplying
#{(\mathcal G_bR_b)(\mathcal G_cR_c)} and using #{R_bR_c=R_{bc}} gives the stated
condition for equality with #{\mathcal G_{bc}R_{bc}}. Invertibility gives
the identity element as well. The last translation relation is the
multiplication rule of the semidirect product.}

\p{For a circle of period #{N\ge3}, take a complex line as fiber.
Let #{P} be exterior parity on the one-axis spin module. It fixes
the scalar basis vector and negates the degree-one vector. The
represented generator exchanges them, so #{P} anticommutes with it.
Put #{(Rf)(y)=P f(-y)}. Site inversion exchanges the forward and
backward shifts, negating their centered difference. The two signs
give #{R D_1 R^{-1}=D_1} at trivial links, and #{R^2=\bbI}.}

\p{Choose a primitive complex #{N}th root #{\zeta}. Write #{M_u}
for multiplication by a scalar field #{u}, set #{h(y)=\zeta^y}
and #{H=M_h}. The gauge transform #{U} of the trivial connection by
#{h} stores the positive link #{h(y+1)h(y)^{-1}=\zeta}.
Its forward pullback uses #{\zeta^{-1}}, and gauge covariance gives
#{D_U=H D_1 H^{-1}}. Inversion sends the stored link to
#{\zeta^{-1}}. The gauge #{h^2} sends this back to #{\zeta}, since
#{h(y+1)^2/h(y)^2=\zeta^2}. The corrected reflection is
#{R'=HRH^{-1}=M_{h^2}R}, as #{h(-y)=h(y)^{-1}}.
Thus #{R'} commutes with #{D_U} and satisfies #{(R')^2=\bbI}, giving
the required group law. Yet #{R T_x R^{-1}=T_{-x}}, so
##{(R' T_x (R')^{-1}f)(y)
  =\frac{h(y)^2}{h(y-x)^2}f(y-x)=\zeta^{2x}f(y-x).}
At #{x=1} the factor differs from one because #{N\ge3}.
This corrected action fails the translation relation.}
}
